# Ootle Template Development Standards

> These standards come from community review of real Ootle templates — in particular the multi-round review of the confidential-rcv-template by Kelketek and sdbondi. If a rule below surprises you, the reference skill (`ootle-template-dev-reference`) explains the failure it prevents.

## Build & Size Discipline

> Every validator stores your template's WASM forever. **Publish cost scales with WASM size**, and so do your users' fees. Size is a first-class constraint, not an afterthought.

- **`[profile.release]` is mandatory.** Without it your template is needlessly large and expensive to publish:
  ```toml
  [profile.release]
  opt-level = 's'     # Optimize for size.
  lto = true          # Enable Link Time Optimization.
  codegen-units = 1   # Reduce number of codegen units to increase optimizations.
  panic = 'abort'     # Abort on panic.
  strip = true        # Strip symbols and debug info.
  ```
- **`crate-type = ["cdylib"]` only — never `["cdylib", "rlib"]`.** Requesting both makes rustc silently drop `-C lto`, so LTO never applies to the WASM (measured: ~76 KB of bloat). If you want pure logic unit-testable, extract it into a **standalone crate** and depend on it from both the template and the tests.
- **Minify before publishing:** `wasm_opt -Oz --enable-bulk-memory` (the `--enable-bulk-memory` flag is required for rustc wasm32 output). A typical template should land around **250 KB** after minification.
- **Scaffold with `tari-ootle-cli`** (`cargo install tari-ootle-cli`) when possible — it sets up the release profile and minification for you.
- Check the compiled size before publishing: `ls -la target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/*.wasm`.

## Dependencies

- **crates.io only, minor-version pins** (e.g. `tari_template_lib = "0.31"`). No git dependencies. Check crates.io for the current minor version before starting a project.
- **Never vendor upstream code** (no `vendor/` directory). If you must patch a crate: fork it, add a `[patch.crates-io]` override + git submodule pointing at your fork, and **open an upstream PR** for the fix. Drop the patch once upstream ships the fix.
- If the review asks for a newer stack: bump `tari_template_lib`, `ootle-rs`, `tari_ootle_transaction`, `tari_template_test_tooling`, and `ootle_byte_type` together — they are released as a set.

## Fees in Client Code

> Hardcoded fee amounts are the #1 client-side review finding. They are either 7x too high (wasted money) or too low (failed transactions).

- **Never hardcode fees.** Estimate with a dry-run: build the transaction → dry-run it → read the estimated fee from the dry-run result → submit with a margin multiplier (2x) for estimation variance.
- The accepted pattern is `build -> dry-run -> read estimated fee -> submit` (worked example in the reference skill).
- Fee amounts are integer "sats-like" units, not TARI. Publish fees for a naive (unoptimized) template can run millions of units (~5.8 TARI for 300+ KB) — size optimization is what brings them down.
- The Wallet Web UI has an "Estimate Fee" button — the easiest way to get an accurate publish estimate.

## Template Design & Security

- **Identity comes from the caller, never from arguments.** Use `CallerContext::transaction_signer_public_key()` for authentication. Accepting a public key as a method argument is spoofable.
- **Ownership is a security boundary, not decoration.** With the default `OwnerRule::OwnedBySigner`, the owner is authorized for *any* resource action — including Mint — regardless of what your `mintable(...)` rule says. A decorative mint rule is not a cap. For permanent caps use `OwnerRule::None` + a mint rule that requires a proof of an NFT badge sealed in a component-internal vault, with locked updaters.
- **Pin configuration at init as an enum, dispatch with one method.** If users can choose between competing modes or algorithms at *call* time (e.g. which calculation method), anyone can compute all variants offline and call whichever gives the result they want. Declare the mode in the constructor and expose a single method that `match`es on it.
- **Privileged roles come from the caller of the constructor** — capture the signer in `new()` rather than hardcoding keys or accepting them as arguments.
- **Vaults are private substates.** The transaction instruction set has no vault-addressing instruction; vaults are reachable only from within their owning component's methods. Don't design APIs that assume clients can touch vaults directly.
- **Stealth outputs need `minimum_value_promise >= 1` when every output must be non-zero** (e.g. distributing one token to each of N recipients). With confidential amounts, output-count checks can't distinguish the intended shape from a degenerate one (e.g. `[2, 0]`); a 0-value output can only be proven with promise 0, so promise-aware construction makes invalid shapes unconstructible rather than merely uncastable.
- **On-chain computation must be deterministic:** fixed-point arithmetic (e.g. scale 10000) instead of floats, deterministic tie-breaks (lowest id/index). Randomness in templates is deterministic anyway — `tari_template_lib::rand::random_bytes`, never the `rand` crate.
- **Define edge semantics explicitly.** Degenerate inputs must have defined, panic-free behavior — an empty state should yield "no result", not an arbitrary one produced by elimination-style loops. Watch for threshold formulas that degenerate at zero input: `floor(x / (n + 1))` computes to 0 when x is 0, so *everything* qualifies — the correct form is `floor(x / (n + 1)) + 1`.

## Testing

> In-process tests with `tari_template_test_tooling` are the primary test mechanism. Testnet/mainnet integration tests are CI-only extras, not the routine workflow.

- **Happy path is not enough.** Every template needs adversarial tests: wrong token type, double-submit/double-spend, use after close/expiration, and nonsense inputs (zero/empty arguments, out-of-range ids, duplicates, wrong-length collections). Use `execute_expect_failure` + `assert_reject_reason`.
- **Control time with `set_virtual_substate`** to test epoch-based rules (deadlines, expirations).
- **Failed transactions roll back completely** — spent UTXOs are returned. You can reuse the same inputs across adversarial attempts, which makes stealth-based adversarial tests cheap.
- **Extract pure algorithms** (computation-heavy logic, state machines) into a standalone crate with no template-ABI dependencies. They become directly unit-testable, and they survive template stack upgrades without changes.
- Keep testnet integration tests minimal and labeled CI-only in comments.

## Client Apps

- **Always set `max_epoch`** on transaction builders — the network rejects transactions without a bounded validity window. Fetch the current epoch from the indexer and add a margin.
- **Two-stealth-input signing binds authorizations to the seal key, not the account key.** When a transaction spends two stealth UTXOs (e.g. spending a stealth asset while paying the fee from another stealth UTXO), authorization signatures must bind to the seal signature's one-time public key P (via `derive_stealth_owner_public_key`), not the account key K. Old `ootle-rs` had this bug — it was invisible with one stealth input and failed with "Invalid transaction signature" with two.
- **Privacy is a property of the whole flow, and templates can't enforce all of it.** Templates never see fee inputs — a client can destroy stealth-recipient anonymity by paying fees from a revealed account. Document the required client behavior (fee-from-stealth) as a hard requirement; consider making it unenforceable-on-chain explicit in the README.
- **Transaction timeout:** the default is 32 seconds, often too short for Esmeralda testnet. Use `connect_with_transaction_timeout(..., Duration::from_secs(120))`.

## Before Merge — Checklist

- [ ] `cargo fmt` run
- [ ] `cargo clippy` clean (watch for `clone_on_copy`, `identity_op`, `too_many_arguments`)
- [ ] `cargo test` passes (unit + adversarial + tooling tests)
- [ ] WASM builds and is minified under budget; size checked
- [ ] No hardcoded fees; dry-run estimation used
- [ ] No vendored code; no git dependencies
- [ ] Docs/README match the code — no stale claims (reviewers check this)
- [ ] Access rules are real: no decorative mint rules, owner rules intentional
- [ ] Edge semantics defined: zero-input, empty-state, expiration, double-spend paths
- [ ] Adversarial tests exist for every public method's failure mode
- [ ] Template metadata (`[package.metadata.tari-template]`) present for the templates explorer